  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 1139 PM MDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nLooking at satellite late this evening, the upper level low is \ncurrently moving out of southern California and into Arizona with\nwidely scattered showers and storms ahead of the trough to our\nsouth. The showers and storms will gradually move north/northeast\nas the surface low tracks northeast. Light showers are starting \nto approach southern Lincoln County as of late this evening. Rain \nchances will increase for the Palmer Divide area late overnight \nthrough early Wednesday morning and the urban corridor by the \nlate morning to early afternoon. Models have continued to trend\nlower for rain amounts in our area, due to a more southern track \nof the upper low and eastward track of the surface low. With the \nsurface low being a bit more east, it's much harder to get the  \nhigher moisture to wrap around into our area. We will have enough \nmoisture for some lighter rain activity and we are still expecting\nrain on Wednesday for many areas south of I-76, but forecast \namounts have decreased and the risk for flooding has decreased. \n\nThe best chance for higher rain amounts in our area will be in our \nsouthern most counties (Lincoln and potentially the Palmer Divide), \nbut the highest rain amounts for this system are expected to \nremain to the southeast of our forecast area. We've left the \nFlood Watch for the southeastern Elbert and southern Lincoln \nCounty zone, but there's still uncertainty on if the heavier\nrainfall will even make it that far north. Short of some unlikely \nmesoscale feature setting up, like an MCV, we're looking at much \nlower rain totals for our area than previously forecast. Overall \nfor our forecast area, expect more of a steady light rain event \nfor areas south of I-76 tomorrow, with a slight chance for one or \ntwo areas to see higher amounts approaching one to two inches.\n\nThis system is forecast to move off to the east late Thursday, with \nupper level ridging building in behind it. The ridging should \nhold through the weekend, bringing warmer and drier conditions. \nHighs are forecast to be above normal Friday through Monday, with \nhighs approaching the low to mid 80s for portions of the plains by\nMonday. Our next chance for rain could accompany an upper level \nshortwave late Monday into Tuesday, but rain chances remain low \n(<30%) for now.",
